<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sauromates II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 174–210 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Tiberius Julius Sauromates II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tiberius Julius Sauromates II was king of the Bosporan Kingdom from about 174 to 210 CE, ruling as a client of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Inscriptions credit him with victories over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraces">Siraces</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tauri">Tauri</a> and with making the sea safe for shipping. His long reign was a time of relative prosperity for the cities of the Bosporus, the high point of the kingdom's Roman period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sauromates-ii.jpg" alt="Sauromates II" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Odysses · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tiberius_Julius_Sauromates_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Inscriptions name Sauromates as the son of the Bosporan king Rhoemetalces, and his predecessor, Eupator, may have been his uncle. His epithets Philocaesar, Philoromaios and Eusebes mean friend of Caesar, friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and pious one. The name Sauromates points to claimed Sarmatian ancestry through marriages to Sarmatian princesses, though it need not mean the family was Sarmatian.</p><p>His dynasty went back to Tiberius Julius Aspurgus and his son Mithridates in the 1st century, son and grandson of the Bosporan ruler Asander and his queen Dynamis. They were related to the ruling house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and descended both from Mithridates VI Eupator of Pontus and from the Roman triumvir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>, through Antonia Tryphaena and her mother Pythodorida of Pontus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Sauromates succeeded Eupator in the early 170s and was a contemporary of the emperors from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a>. His coins, which name him King Sauromates in Greek, suggest devotion to the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a>. An inscription found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/don-river">Tanais</a> celebrates his victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> and the Sirachi in 193, at the end of a campaign that may have begun in 186.</p><p>That campaign may have brought on a financial crisis, and from 186 he reformed the coinage over a decade, gradually reducing the weight of the bronze coins while putting more gold staters into circulation. In the last decade of the 2nd century his coins often carried the portrait of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a>; whether at the emperor's command or by his own choice is not known. His wife's name has not survived, but their son Rhescuporis III succeeded him in 210 or 211.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sauromates II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
